kustinMillerComplex -- Compute Kustin-Miller resolution of the unprojection of I in J
*************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        kustinMillerComplex(I,J,W)
        kustinMillerComplex(cI,cJ,W)
  * Inputs:
      * J, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, in a positively graded
        polynomial ring R
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, contained in J
      * cI, a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, resolution of I
      * cJ, a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, resolution of J
      * W, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, over the
        same *note coefficientRing: (Macaulay2Doc)coefficientRing, as R with
        one variable T.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Verbose: kustinMillerComplex_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbose=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value 0, Option to print intermediate data
  * Outputs:
      * a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, 

Description
===========

Compute Kustin-Miller resolution of the unprojection of I in J (or equivalently
of the image J' of J in R/I) with unprojection variable T.

We have the following setup:

Assume R is a *note PolynomialRing: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing, over a field,
the degrees of all variables positive and $I \subset J \subset R$ two
homogeneous ideals of R such that R/I and R/J are Gorenstein and
dim(R/J)=dim(R/I)-1.

Let R/I(k_1) and R/J(k_2) be the canonical modules of R/I and R/J respectively.
We require k_1 - k_2, that is, the degree of the unprojection variable, to be
positive.

For a description of this resolution and how it is computed see

J. Boehm, S. Papadakis: Implementing the Kustin-Miller complex construction,
http://arxiv.org/abs/1103.2314



It is also possible to specify minimal resolutions of I and J.

The function *note kustinMillerComplex: kustinMillerComplex, returns a chain
complex over a new polynomial ring S with the same *note coefficientRing:
(Macaulay2Doc)coefficientRing, as R and the variables of R and W, where
degree(T) = *note degree: (Macaulay2Doc)degree, *note unprojectionHomomorphism:
unprojectionHomomorphism,(I,J) = k_1-k_2.

To avoid printing the variables of this ring when printing the chain complex
just give a name to the ring (e.g., do S = *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)ring, cc
to call it S).

We illustrate the Kustin-Miller complex construction at the example described
in Section 5.5 of

Papadakis, Kustin-Miller unprojection with complexes,  J. Algebraic Geometry 13
(2004) 249-268, http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0111195





