  * Usage: 
        unprojectionHomomorphism(I,J)
  * Inputs:
      * J, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, with R/J Gorenstein
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, contained in J with R/I
        Gorenstein and dim(R/J)=dim(R/I)-1.
  * Outputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, 

Description
===========

Compute the deformation associated to the unprojection of $I \subset J$ (or
equivalently of $J'\subset R/I$ where $R$ = *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)ring, $I$
and $J'=$*note substitute: (Macaulay2Doc)substitute,$(J,R/I)$), i.e., a
homomorphism

$\phi : J' \to R/I$

such that the unprojected ideal $U\subset R[T]$ is the inverse image of

$U' = (T*u - \phi(u)  |  u \in J' ) \subset (R/I)[T]$

under the natural map $R[T]\to(R/I)[T]$.

The result is represented by a matrix $f$ with *note source:
(Macaulay2Doc)source, $f$ = J' and *note target: (Macaulay2Doc)target, $f$ =
(R/I)^1.



